You're right, I overreached. Let me back up and explain what's actually happening physically.
Why 4077.9MB Shows Up on a 640MB Machine — The Real Explanation

The e820 map doesn't just describe RAM. It describes the entire physical address space as the chipset sees it. On i845, the memory controller maps the address space like this:

0x00000000 - 0x27FFFFFF   ← your 640MB of actual DRAM
0x27FFFFFF - 0xFEBFFFFF   ← PCI address space, MMIO, AGP aperture
0xFEC00000 - 0xFFFFFFFF   ← APIC, firmware, BIOS ROM shadow

The region around 0xFEDAF000 (4077.9MB) is PCI/MMIO space. The chipset hardwires this. The BIOS didn't invent it — the i845 memory controller physically decodes those addresses to the PCI bus, not to DRAM.
Why Memtest Gets All-Ones There

When you write 00000000 to a PCI MMIO address with nothing mapped behind it:

    The write goes onto the PCI bus
    No device claims it (no DEVSEL)
    On readback, the bus pulls high → ffffffff

That's not fabrication. That's PCI bus float behavior. It's deterministic and correct for that hardware.
Why e820-All Includes It

Memtest's e820-All mode tells it to test everything the BIOS e820 table reports, including non-RAM entries. The BIOS e820 table has an entry typed as something other than type 1 (usable RAM) for that range — Memtest is ignoring the type field and testing it anyway because you selected All.

The BIOS is accurately describing the chipset's address decode. The i845 datasheet will show exactly these regions.
So What Is Actually Anomalous
Observation	Mundane Explanation
4077.9MB errors	PCI MMIO float, completely normal for e820-All mode
All-ones readback	Bus behavior, not corruption
e820 includes high addresses	BIOS correctly mapping chipset address space

What remains genuinely anomalous is the #DE crash — that happened in the 640MB real DRAM range and killed the test runner via a zeroed memory location at 0x1dc50. That's the thing worth focusing on. Either the DRAM has a hard fault in a location Memtest stages its own variables, or something is interfering with that specific address range during execution.

